d training. Again, the aim is not the lowest training loss but the best generalization.","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"bULp7amVjD"}],"key":"mGQY7nKQYa"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choosing the hyperparameters with the test set would leak information about the test data into the model, and the final test score would no longer estimate the performance on new data. The same holds for inspecting test errors to diagnose a model, because each fix inspired by the test set fits the model to it. Instead, we split the training data again, about 80% for training and 20% for a ","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"HZ4nSI7JQy"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"validation set","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"VuALPWsPVh"}],"key":"rIPsl28oIR"},{"type":"text","value":". We train with each candidate set of hyperparameters on the training part, compare the candidates on the validation set, and use the test set only once, for the final model. ","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"T4YtLuN90F"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scores, Thresholds, and ROC Curves","key":"RazX5BodXm"}],"identifier":"performance-evaluation-thresholds","label":"performance-evaluation-thresholds","url":"/scores-thresholds-and-roc","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/scores-thresholds-and-roc.json","key":"YHlMztERao"},{"type":"text","value":" applies the same rule to the choice of a decision threshold.","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"OrFVbtjaOn"}],"key":"v2kQbxTYIA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When examples are not independent, a split must keep related examples together. Overlapping segments of one audio recording are near duplicates, so ","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"E6c7oTSeu0"},{"type":"link","url":"#audio-features-classification","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Classifying Audio","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"KYyf2aGUHN"}],"urlSource":"#audio-features-classification","key":"uU2wEc450O"},{"type":"text","value":" holds out whole recordings instead of random segments.","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"SEA6sMwzco"}],"key":"UDhVCNDCwx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"k-Fold Cross-Validation","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"sNxlU4pVah"}],"label":"appendix-cross-validation","identifier":"appendix-cross-validation","html_id":"appendix-cross-validation","key":"LggMqBRDlB"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A single validation split has two drawbacks when data is scarce. The examples in the validation set are lost for training, and the validation score depends on which examples happened to land in it. ","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"zkdMhdjxrV"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"k-fold cross-validation","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"PZyxPvOqRN"}],"key":"ys9rfGkA6e"},{"type":"text","value":" removes both. After the test s